course: dictionary
lesson: 7815
title: Quicksand: a trap, real or not
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Quicksand is loose, wet sand that you can sink into when you step on it. Context clues help you see what kind of trouble it is. When you read about quicksand near a river, you know it means real sand. When it is used in a sentence about a hard problem, it means something else.
explain: «Quicksand can trap your legs, but a person floats in it and does not sink over their head; you get free by moving slowly and leaning back.» Trap means hold you so you cannot get out. The clues here are floats and moving slowly, which tell you that the real danger is panic, not drowning under the sand.
explain: Writers also use quicksand to mean a situation that is hard to escape, even when no sand is involved. Ask yourself: is there real sand in the sentence? If not, the writer may be using quicksand in a figurative way, which means in a picture-like sense.
fact: Quicksand is loose, wet sand. «Quicksand» is a noun, and its plural is «quicksands».
fact: You find quicksand near rivers, beaches and marshes.
fact: In real life you rarely sink above your waist.
fact: If you are caught, stay calm, lie back and move slowly to get free.
fact: Figuratively, quicksand means a situation that is hard to escape.
exercise: What is quicksand? | A type of fizzy drink | A dry desert with cactus plants | A hard rock on a mountain | *Loose, wet sand that you can sink into
exercise: Where would you most likely find quicksand? | Inside a school library | *Near a river, beach or marsh | On top of a skyscraper | In a shop
exercise: Figuratively, what can quicksand mean? | A quiet room | *A situation that is hard to escape | A kind of sweet | A fast train
test: If you get caught in quicksand, what should you do? | Struggle hard and jump up | Dig a hole with your hands | Shout and run straight to the middle | *Stay calm, lie back and move slowly
test: Read «Quicksand can trap your legs, but a person floats in it.» What does this tell you? | Quicksand is only found in films | You always sink right under and drown | *In real life you do not usually sink over your head | Quicksand is harder than rock
exam: Which sentence uses quicksand in the figurative sense? | Quicksand is found on beaches | *She felt caught in quicksand, with every choice making things worse | Explorers feared the quicksand near the marsh | The quicksand dried out under the hot sun
